{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1170919-custom-paradox-conditions","id":1170919,"title":"Custom Paradox Conditions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-12-27T06:32:37-08:00","id":1170919,"title":"Custom Paradox Conditions","content":"Greetings one and all,<br /> <br /> Unless my search-fu has entirely failed me, we have not had a good thread for sharing and compiling the community's custom Paradox Conditions, which is a great shame considering how creative and weird you can get with them. This is what this thread is for. Below you will find some of mine and I will steadily update this thread as I properly write out the various Paradox Conditions I have used in my games (most of them currently only short notes). Feel very free to post your own. I may end up compiling a Google Doc with collected submissions for ease of reference once we have a good number together. <hr /><br /> <b>NB:</b> Each Condition names the most relevant Arcana for that Condition. Do not feel bound or limited by these. The unreality of the Abyss is not so rulebound as our meek Fallen selves.<br /> <br /> <b>Abyssal Agony (Life, Mind, Time)</b><br /> Unnatural energies course through the Awakened\u2019s body, filling it with searing pain not of this world. The mage suffers Wound Penalties equal to the successes earned on the Paradox roll, which stack with mundane Wound Penalties. Merits and supernatural abilities that reduce Wound Penalties can be used to negate the penalties from this Condition. While this Condition could theoretically levy penalties greater than -5 at that point the mage would likely just pass out from unbearable pain anyway.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage fails a number of rolls because of this Condition equal to Paradox successes. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Abyssal Hunger (Life, Mind, Spirit)</b><br /> The character experiences a ravishing hunger for some odd or horrifying substance. This substance could be everything, but is always unsettling and unnatural. The mage might crave trash, spoiled food, widow\u2019s tears, wilted rose petals or anything else conceivable that is weird and unwholesome.<br /> While she has this Condition the character cannot gain nourishment from anything but the subject of her unnatural craving and will suffer the ill effects of deprivation if she does not pursue her hunger. This could lead to dice penalties or even damage if the Condition persists long enough. When confronted with a clear opportunity to satisfy her craving the character must pass a Resolve + Composure - Paradox successes roll, spend a Willpower point or indulge immediately.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The character gorges herself on the substance to the point of significant negative consequences (whether directly derived from the nature of the substance or the act of being witnessed devouring it in large quantities). The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Abyssal Infection (Life, Death, Time)</b><br /> The nature of the Abyss infects the mage\u2019s system, causing wounds to fester in inexplicable ways, deprivation to take a major toll on their body and poisons to ravage his body beyond what science could explain. For a number of times equal to the successes scored on the Paradox roll, every time the mage suffers any amount of damage that damage is resistant damage of its type. Damage that is already resistant does not count against the resolution of this Condition.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage suffers a number of instances of damage equal to successes on the Paradox roll. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Abyssal Oath (Fate)</b><br /> The willworker has accumulated a grave debt to the Abyss and it is time to pay her dues. The mage is forced into an Oath to the Abyss, the words of the Oath inadvertently echoing through her mind in her own voice. These Oaths usually involve performing some task or abstaining from something. This often means some grotesque, horrible or just nonsensical act involving one or more of the mage\u2019s sympathetic connections. Other times, the demand furthers the interests of the Abyss more directly. In any case, the mage is under the effect of a boon (p. 134) when working towards the objective and under a hex (ibid.) when doing anything else. The Potency of the hex and boon are equal to the Paradox successes that caused this Condition. Any attempts, mundane or supernatural, of altering the Oath automatically fail. When choosing the Oath the Storyteller should take care that the Oath can be realistically fulfilled before this Condition lapses according to the mage\u2019s Wisdom. Wise mage will often be forced to pursue more drawn out, elaborate goals while less Wise often have to perform acts more immediately destructive to sane reality. Many Awakened who end up suffering this Condition find they are unable to bring themselves to fulfil it. Fortunately, the Old Man may agree to alternative arrangements.<br /> <b>Resolution</b>: Fulfil the Oath. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Aura of Untruth (Prime, Mind, Fate)</b><br /> Truth and lies twist around a mage so contaminated by the Abyss and everyone he meets will feel it. Everything the mage communicates is immediately disbelieved by others. The subject becomes almost impossible to believe, even against his best attempts at appearing honest and truthful. If the mage makes a compelling case (requiring proof) or the truth of what he says is plainly obvious others must still test Resolve + Composure - Paradox successes to force themselves to belief him. If this roll is failed even obvious things stated by the mage cannot be believed. In Sleepers and Sleepwalkers this may lead to irreconcilable cognitive dissonance, which may then lead to Breaking Points.<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The mage shares a truth and is then disbelieved, leading to severe consequences for the listener (this may include incurring severe Breaking Points). The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Body-ravaging Paradox (Life, Mind, Space)</b><br /> The Abyss forces itself into the mage and plays havoc with her body and its natural functions, causing complete numbness or uncontrollable reactions. For every success on the Paradox roll the mage suffers one of the following Tilts: Arm Wrack, Leg Wrack, Blinded, Deafened, Sick. Note that each Tilt (except Sick) only applies to one specific body part, so a mage can suffer two instances of Leg Wrack, one for each leg, or suffer Blinded twice, one for each eye. A die roll can be used to randomize which Tilt is used or the Storyteller can decide.<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The mage scourges her body, dealing at least an amount of Lethal damage equal to Paradox successes<b>. </b>The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Dance of the Annunaki (Fate, Life, Mind)</b><br /> The mage has allowed the Abyss egress into his very being, letting its insidious influence play him like a puppet on a string. Every Scene the Storyteller has a number of dice equal to the successes scored on the Paradox roll. This dice pool can be rolled once every Scene and if any successes are scored the Storyteller can force the character to perform an Instant Action with a number of successes equal to the successes rolled by the Storyteller. The mage might suddenly punch someone he is talking to, notice a random thing, recall a seemingly unrelated memory or suddenly blurt out a strangely seductive one-liner. Only casting a spell or leveraging an Attainment is beyond the grasp of the Annunaki. If an Exceptional Success is scored on the associated roll this should be exceptionally detrimental to the mage instead of beneficial.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> This Condition severely complicates the mage\u2019s life or endangers them. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Fleshwarp (Life)</b><br /> Paradox has twisted the mage\u2019s body, turning it into unnatural shapes. The willworker experiences a number of changes equal to the successes on the Paradox roll. The Storyteller can mine the Transform Life spell (p. 150) for examples for possible changes, but transformations from this Condition tend to be even more outlandish, freakish and often either useless or actively hazardous to the mage and her environment. Yet sometimes they can be very situationally useful. Seeing the transformed mage will usually mean at least Social penalties if not outright Breaking Points and panic. If the transformations require Potency assume that it is equal to the successes rolled on the Paradox roll.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage scourges her body, dealing at least an amount of Lethal damage equal to Paradox successes; in the case of major Paradoxes (3+ successes) this may instead be Aggravated damage as limbs need to be removed wholesale. The mage physically hurts someone in his last three Health boxes with a transformed body part. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Out of Time (Time)</b><br /> The mage has been shunted outside the normal flow of time by Paradox. Until this Condition is resolved or lapses, the subject is removed from the Present and thrown forwards or backwards in time a number of Scenes equal to the successes on the Paradox roll. Whether any changes to the Past or a potential Future caused by the mage actually stick is up to the whims of the Abyss. Unlike normal time travel, as through the Time Arcanum, both the Past or Future he inhabits and the Present are moving forwards at the same time, which may lead to situations like sudden changes in the Present as the mage affects the Past in a significant way (possibly causing Breaking Points in witnesses). Some Wise who have experienced this Condition posit that they have not actually been to the Past or Future at all, but an Annunaki of Time creating a world for them and through them invade the Fallen World.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage causes a significant change to occur. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Tainted Essence (Death, Mind, Spirit)</b><br /> The mage\u2019s Essence and Nimbus have been tainted with the putrid extracts of the Abyss. Ephemeral entities can smell the taint on the willworker and will try to avoid her. The mage suffers a penalty equal to successes on the Paradox roll to all Social Actions with ephemeral entities. If an ephemeral entity is forced to partake of the mage\u2019s Essence (whether by starvation or coercion) or is the subject of a powerful spell from her its nature is twisted by the Abyss. This can include changing Virtues, Vices, Aspirations, Bans, Banes, Influences and Numina in nonsensical ways. As a rule of thumb, if the final Potency of a spell is greater than the entity\u2019s Rank its nature is warped in this way, one Trait changing per excess Potency. In extreme cases the entity may be fully transmogrified into a gulmoth or acamoth. Causing such changes is at least an Act of Hubris at Understanding level. Severe changes are an Act of Hubris at Falling level.<br /> At the Storyteller\u2019s discretion this Condition may apply to only one specific kind of ephemeral entity. Resolving this Condition by meditation or cleansing (see below) requires at least a number of successes equal to three times the successes scored on the Paradox roll.<br /> <b>Resolution: </b>The mage purifies herself by successfully mediating at a Hallow, Demesne or similar location or performs a similar kind of cleansing ritual. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-27T06:51:03-08:00","id":1170923,"title":"","content":"I think these are a good start, but I\u2019d like to point out that the Paradox Conditions in the book are typically Resolved by overcoming the imposition of the Abyss. For instance the one that forces you to spend extra Reach (Abyssal Imago) requires you to cast a spell without risking Paradox in spite of the increase in Reach you have to employ. So I think a number of these Conditions you\u2019ve come up with should probably have the Resolution mechanic reworked.<br /> <br /> For instance Aura of Untruth could be resolved by managing to succeed in convincing someone of the truth in spite of the penalties.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-27T10:33:23-08:00","id":1170984,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1170919-custom-paradox-conditions?p=1170923#post1170923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think these are a good start, but I\u2019d like to point out that the Paradox Conditions in the book are typically Resolved by overcoming the imposition of the Abyss. For instance the one that forces you to spend extra Reach (Abyssal Imago) requires you to cast a spell without risking Paradox in spite of the increase in Reach you have to employ. So I think a number of these Conditions you\u2019ve come up with should probably have the Resolution mechanic reworked.<br /> <br /> For instance Aura of Untruth could be resolved by managing to succeed in convincing someone of the truth in spite of the penalties.</div> </div> </div> </div>A fair objection. However, as <b>Abyssal Nimbus</b> (MtA: 117) and <b>Monster </b>(DE: 81) show, this is not a fast rule. The former's Resolution Conditions is directly in furtherance of the Abyss and the Wise thing to do is to minimize the damage (usually by cloistering yourself away and keeping your Nimbus in check) and wait for the lapse. The latter does not even have a 'positive' Resolution Condition. No matter how it ends, a bit of reality has frayed (although I will grant you that it really reads like a Condition that should only happen as a result of a Released Paradox affecting a subject, instead of a Contained Paradox, even if it does not specifically say so).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":272,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","user":"saibot","date":". 12-27-2017, 01:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-27T10:43:10-08:00","id":1170993,"title":"","content":"Fair enough.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-27T11:53:37-08:00","id":1171015,"title":"","content":"I noticed Matter is absent. Maybe something about contaminating objects touched (or a Mage's Dedicated Tools) with Abyssal impossibility, such as switching the equipment bonus to odd subjects, or cursing the item with an auto-cast spell whenever a Sleeper (since they possess a shard of the Abyss) touches it? Actually, the last is probably more fitting for Prime.<br /> <br /> Actually, Forces too. Maybe a subjective Extreme Environment (as wind starts cutting like knives, or the Mage stops interacting properly with things like thermal exchange)?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 12-27-2017, 02:55 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-28T07:07:59-08:00","id":1171224,"title":"","content":"I very much agree with Vent0, so I prioritized writing out Conditions for the neglected Arcana.<br /> <br /> <b>Dissolving Touch (Matter)</b><br /> Even inert matter is not save from the corrupting caress of the Outer Dark and with this Condition the mage becomes the carrier of this corruption. The mage\u2019s touch dissolves all solid objects. The Durability of any touched item decreases by 1 point per Turn. Once Durability is at 0, it loses 1 point of Structure per Turn, until it is fully liquefied into some unidentifiable dark ooze. Not even his clothes are exempt from this. If the Paradox roll that inflicted this Condition scored 3 or more successes this effect ignores Durability entirely.<br /> Note that Supernal Artifacts and objects made from Perfected materials are immune to this effect. Other supernatural items are not immune but highly resistant, only suffering a loss of Durability or Structure every Scene, not every Turn.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage dissolves an object important to him. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Freak Energies (Forces)</b><br /> Paradox causes the forces of the universe to go haywire around the mage. When this Condition is gained the Storyteller must choose one natural force, when the mage interacts with that force in any way its intensity is a number of levels higher or lower equal to successes gained on the Paradox roll (see the \u201cTranform Energy\u201d table, MtA 2e: 146). This can cause electrical devices to immediately short out when touched by her, perhaps explosively, as the voltage of a main line runs through them. Or she inadvertently deafens others when speaking. The Storyteller may change the direction, up or down, of this effect every Scene.<br /> Alternatively, instead of choosing one force, this Condition can affect a number of forces equal to successes on the Paradox roll all at once. However, in that case the intensity may only be moved up or down one step, but may be adjusted in different directions for different forces.<br /> Note that this Condition can never directly harm or kill the mage (as for example from her body heating up to the point of self-immolation), but may very well cause harm as a result of her interacting with her environment. Bystanders are not so lucky.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage grounds herself by successfully mediating at a Hallow, Demesne or similar location or performs a similar kind of grounding ritual. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.<br /> <br /> <b>Spatially Unstable (Space)</b><br /> The mage has lost her linear connection with space to Paradox and is torn to and fro. Every new Scene she is teleported to a different location, often but not always places she has sympathetic connections to. This teleport can be resisted by spending a Willpower point and succeeding on a roll of Wits + Resolve - successes on the Paradox roll. If that roll is successful the mage stays at her current location.<br /> <b>Resolution:</b> The mage successfully resists the teleport a number of times equal to successes on the Paradox roll. The mage allows the Condition to lapse after the specified time.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Politeness is the lubricant of social intercourse.","edited":{"userid":272,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","user":"saibot","date":". 12-28-2017, 10:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/272-saibot","name":"saibot","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar272_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1063"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-12-28T09:18:40-08:00","id":1171248,"title":"","content":"Freak Energies: No electrical conduction = now your touch screen doesn't work.<br /> <br /> Other ideas:<br /> Spirit - animating some random thing you own. Your refrigerator really <i>is</i> running away.<br /> Matter - you stop interacting with certain materials. Have to use plastic-ware because all metal passes right through you.<br /> Space - sympathy roulette. Suddenly, you really love your toaster, and are merely fond of your SO. <br /> Or mistaken identity. You are suddenly prime suspect or mistaken for being famous.<br /> Mind - astral solipsism. Going on an Abyssal dream quest.<br /> Prime - lying Mage Sight. Why does that stray dog reek of Life and Fate magic? Better investigate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 12-28-2017, 01:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}